-- BMC bound hunt: a bug that lives at a specific depth.
--
-- This model is a plain counter 0 -> 1 -> ... -> 15 -> 0 -> ... A "bug" flag
-- is true exactly when cnt = 12. The safety spec `INVARSPEC !bug` is therefore
-- FALSE, and the shortest counterexample has length 12 (it takes 12 steps to
-- drive cnt from 0 to 12).
--
-- The lesson — Bounded Model Checking (BMC) vs. unbounded model checking:
--   * Plain `NuSMV bmc_depth.smv` does full (BDD) reachability and finds the
--     violation immediately, reporting the spec false.
--   * BMC only looks `k` steps deep. In nuXmv:
--         read_model -i bmc_depth.smv ; go_bmc
--         check_invar_bmc -k 11      -- NO counterexample (bug is deeper than 11)
--         check_invar_bmc -k 12      -- counterexample found at depth 12
--     Hunt for the smallest k that exposes the bug. That k is the bug's depth.
--
-- This is the flip side of Day-3 induction: induction proves a property for ALL
-- depths at once; BMC only refutes (or fails to refute) up to depth k. A passing
-- BMC run at depth k is NOT a proof unless k reaches the system's diameter.
MODULE main
VAR
  cnt : 0..15;            -- a simple wrapping counter

ASSIGN
  init(cnt) := 0;
  next(cnt) := case
    cnt = 15 : 0;         -- wrap around at the top
    TRUE     : cnt + 1;   -- otherwise just count up
  esac;

DEFINE bug := (cnt = 12);   -- the "bad" condition, reached only at step 12

-- SAFETY (DELIBERATELY FALSE): claims the bug never happens. The shortest
-- counterexample has length 12 — use it to calibrate how deep BMC must search.
INVARSPEC !bug;
